# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Copied into `dist/` verbatim by `expo export --platform web`, exactly like `_redirects`.
# Cloudflare reads it at deploy time; nothing in the bundle is aware of it, which is the
# point — these are the controls that survive a compromised dependency.
#
# The threat this file exists for is named in `lib/supabase.ts`: the session token lives in
# `sessionStorage`, readable by any script on this origin, and the argument that this is
# acceptable rests on the origin being a small target — no HTML rendered from user input,
# no third-party script, no WebView. `script-src 'self'` is what turns that argument from a
# description of today's code into something a browser enforces.

/*
  # `style-src` needs 'unsafe-inline' and cannot avoid it. React Native Web has no
  # stylesheet file: it builds CSS rules at runtime from the style objects in every
  # component and injects them into a `<style>` element it creates itself. There is no
  # build step that could hash or nonce them, because the rules do not exist until the
  # component renders. `dist/index.html` also carries Expo's own inline `#expo-reset`
  # block. The exposure is real but narrow — CSS injection needs an injection point, and
  # `script-src 'self'` is what denies the one that would matter.
  #
  # `script-src 'self'` with no 'unsafe-eval': verified against a real export. The bundle
  # contains two `eval` call sites and neither runs in a browser — uuid's is guarded on
  # `typeof window == 'undefined'`, and Metro's split-bundle loader is unreachable while
  # the export is a single bundle (`web bundles (1)`). Adding a dynamic `import()` would
  # produce a second chunk that Metro loads with `eval`, and it would fail closed under
  # this policy. That is the right failure — loud, at the first deploy — but know it is
  # the tripwire.
  #
  # `img-src` carries `data:` and `blob:` for the photo path (§16): the picker and
  # `expo-image-manipulator` hand back object URLs before anything is uploaded, and the
  # signed URLs the Feed renders come back from Storage on the Supabase host.
  #
  # `connect-src` is the whole network surface. Every request this app makes goes through
  # the one `createClient` in `lib/supabase.ts` — PostgREST, Auth, Storage and Functions
  # are all paths under `EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`, not separate hosts. Fonts are bundled
  # as local assets by `@expo-google-fonts` (16 `.ttf` files land in `dist/assets/`), so
  # nothing is fetched from `fonts.gstatic.com` despite that string appearing in the
  # bundle's package metadata. The one host deliberately left out is `exp.host`: minting
  # an Expo push token is guarded on `Device.isDevice` and refused for any platform that
  # is not ios/android (`lib/notifications.ts`), and the call is wrapped in a `catch` that
  # answers `null` — so a browser that somehow reached it fails the way it already fails.
  #
  # The wildcard in `*.supabase.co` is a deliberate loosening: the project ref is only
  # known at build time and this file is static. It admits any Supabase project, which is
  # a far smaller set than any host.
  #
  # OAuth still works under `default-src 'self'`. `signInWithProvider` navigates the
  # document to the provider on web (see `lib/auth.ts`) and top-level navigation is not
  # governed by CSP fetch directives.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.supabase.co; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  # A `.ttf` served as `text/plain` and sniffed into something executable is the classic
  # version of this; there is no case in this app where guessing a type is wanted.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  # Paths here are addresses of a Family's data — `/board/<id>`, `/tile/<id>`. Nothing
  # off-origin is loaded, so no legitimate request needs a Referer, and the one navigation
  # that leaves (to the OAuth provider) does not want the board id going with it.
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  # One year, and deliberately **without** `preload`. Preloading is a submission to a list
  # baked into browser binaries and is slow and awkward to reverse; `includeSubDomains` on
  # a custom apex domain likewise commits every subdomain the owner might later add to
  # HTTPS. Both are the right defaults for this site, but they are the owner's to widen.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  # `X-Frame-Options` is omitted on purpose: `frame-ancestors 'none'` above says the same
  # thing to every browser that has shipped this decade, and two headers disagreeing is a
  # maintenance trap rather than defence in depth.
